# TODO - Working Blooket writes and browser fallback

## Objective

Validate actual action payloads and responses, select supported transports
before mutation, and reconcile ambiguous outcomes without replay.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when the product can create a set and add or edit admitted
questions through a working Blooket transport, with the resulting quiz verified
from fresh remote state. A browser implementation is enough to ship this
functionality. HTTP writes are an optional optimization: do not postpone a
working browser path to reverse-engineer an unverified private HTTP response.

Select any supported transport before mutation. If a write might have happened,
reconcile before retrying or using another transport; never create duplicates
merely because a request timed out. Demonstrate actual product-owned Create Set
and Add Question behavior plus focused failure tests. Leave speculative HTTP
coverage and broad compatibility exploration to ongoing hardening.

## Implementation notes

The candidate helper still supports only text create/add. Tests pin the observed
update set/question action hashes as identifiers only; those hashes do not
implement editing, and no HTTP write is activated by these request fixtures.

Implement concrete write surfaces using the session/read adapters from task 08.
Confirm authentication, current build, account capabilities, and the exact
payload before a mutation; preserve every human-stop state.

Prefer HTTP only for an admitted, currently verified operation. Use the browser
fallback for unsupported operations before sending a mutation. A timeout or
ambiguous primary result must enter journal reconciliation, never an immediate
fallback or retry that can duplicate a quiz/question.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-10, the serialized Add Question page opener gained a
synchronous final check of the exact expected prior question-card
sequence at the click boundary. The host now supplies its admitted next
question number to that same injected function, so a teacher or provider
card inserted after the host's two read-only preflights prevents opening
the write form. Synthetic page and host tests verify contiguous cards,
hidden or duplicate indices, invalid numbers, and exact argument binding.

On 2026-10-10, the extension worker's native-browser deadline was
also aligned with the new bounded 60-second broker write lease. It now
permits 57 seconds of owned browser operations before requiring a stop,
leaving transport margin and retaining the separate eight-second read
budget. Worker integration tests simulate an in-budget write after the
former 27-second cutoff and an expired write at 65 seconds; neither
requires duplicate Save clicks or live provider access.

On 2026-10-10, the local browser bridge write lease increased from
30 to 60 seconds to accommodate bounded Create Set navigation and the
new fresh-document saved-state read-back without expiring an active Save
while the provider hydrates. The ordinary read lease remains ten seconds;
explicit test overrides remain authoritative. Synthetic broker tests
accept one delayed but in-budget receipt and reject a completion exactly
at expiry; timeout still requires journal reconciliation, not replay.

On 2026-10-10, Create Set confirmation gained a separate fresh-document
read-back. After matching two saved metadata observations on the new Edit
route, the extension refuses to reload over teacher work, requires a
changed native document origin, and verifies the exact redirect ID and
submitted title/description twice again. A redirect with optimistic but
unpersisted metadata, a stale reload, changed account content, or a
human-selected route cannot produce a saved-set receipt. Synthetic host
regressions exercise those failures without any real account mutation.

On 2026-10-10, Add Question also began reading the exact prior
question-number collection twice, with an event-loop yield, before
opening its form. A newly appeared or malformed card between preflight
and modal acquisition now blocks the write without a click. Synthetic
regressions verify no form opens and no mutation begins on this race.

On 2026-10-10, Add Question stopped accepting a same-document card as
saved remote confirmation. After a single Save and exact owned-modal
inspection, the host checks that no teacher draft can be lost, reloads
only its admitted Edit tab, confirms a distinct native document epoch,
and repeats the full question and optional image identity inspection.
Synthetic tests prove optimistic cards disappearing after reload, an
unchanged document epoch, and new human draft activity cannot produce a
receipt or another submit.

On 2026-10-10, Add Question post-submit confirmation began requiring
the exact contiguous question sequence through the newly added number,
not merely the presence of that number among unrelated cards. After
closing its owned inspection panel, the host repeats the collection
check to reject concurrent edits. Synthetic browser tests confirm that
